\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cross-family SFT-injection results (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) use seed$=$42 data across six models from five independent families. Within-family IRT analyses (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}--\ref{sec:domain_differential}) use $J=16$ Qwen2.5 models (8$\times$1.5B + 8$\times$3B); domain-level contamination estimates $\gamma_d$ (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}) are reported at $J=16$ in the text and $J=13$ in Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}.

\subsection{Dose-Response Patterns}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{figures/fig_baseline_vulnerability.pdf}
\caption{Baseline MMLU accuracy vs.\ contamination response at d100 ($N\!=\!6$; observational association, not a validated threshold).}
\label{fig:baseline_vulnerability}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Observed dose-response patterns under controlled SFT injection (seed$=$42, MMLU). $\Delta$acc: d000$\to$d100 change (95\% CI from item-level paired bootstrap, $B=10{,}000$). Stab.: bootstrap classification stability. Classifications are robust to threshold hyperparameter choice (5/5 original families at 100\% stability across 30 reasonable $(\tau,\sigma)$ combinations; Appendix~\ref{sec:classification_sensitivity}).}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\footnotesize
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llcccccc}
\toprule
Pattern & Model & d000 Acc. & $\Delta$acc & 95\% CI & Peak & Stab. & Repl. \\
\midrule
Immune & Phi-4 14.7B & 76.4\% & $\pm$0.5pp & [$-$0.60, $+$0.26] & negl. & 100\% & \checkmark \\
Collapse & Qwen-14B & 69.4\% & $-$9.4pp\textsuperscript{$\ddagger$} & [$-$12.74, $-$9.0] & --- & 100\% & $\triangle$ \\
V-recovery & Qwen-7B & 66.1\% & $-$1.8pp & [$-$2.36, $-$1.22] & $-$3.1\textsuperscript{d025} & 99.6\% & $\triangle$ \\
Benefit & Gemma-2 9B & 61.9\% & $+$3.8pp & [$+$3.18, $+$4.33] & mono. & 76.6\% & \checkmark \\
Benefit & Falcon3 7B & 58.8\% & $+$4.2pp & [$+$3.60, $+$4.79] & mono. & 96.1\% & \checkmark \\
Benefit & OLMo-2 7B & 53.8\% & $+$3.2pp\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & [$+$2.73, $+$3.71] & sat.\textsuperscript{d050} & 100\% & \checkmark \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{8}{l}{\scriptsize $^{\ddagger}$Cross-seed range $-$9.4 to $-$25.0pp (s42/s123/s0); see Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}.} \\
\multicolumn{8}{l}{\scriptsize $^{\dagger}$Seed-sensitive: s42 $+$3.2pp, s123 $-$0.6pp, s0 $+$1.4pp; cross-seed CI crosses zero. See \S\ref{sec:cross_seed}.} \\
\multicolumn{8}{l}{\scriptsize \checkmark\,replicated across independent families; $\triangle$\,preliminary (Qwen2.5-specific, pending replication).}
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Applying Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification} to seed$=$42 accuracy trajectories yields a spectrum of dose-response patterns (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}, Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}), not a uniform inflation effect.

\paragraph{Replication status.}
Two patterns replicate across independent families (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}). \textit{Immune}: Phi-4 ($\pm$0.5pp), confirmed out-of-sample by LLaMA-3.1 ($-$0.69pp). \textit{Benefit}: three families gain $+$3.2 to $+$4.2pp (Gemma-2, Falcon3, OLMo-2 borderline; \S\ref{sec:cross_seed}). Two patterns are Qwen2.5-specific and await independent replication: Qwen-14B \textit{collapses} ($-$9.4 to $-$25.0pp across seeds)---3$\times$ more fragile than 7B despite higher baseline---this within-family divergence may reflect catastrophic interference at larger scale---while Qwen-7B shows \textit{V-recovery}. ARC direction is consistent for 5/6 models (Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}); domain-level effects vary up to 1.8$\times$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:domain_decomposition}).

\paragraph{Out-of-sample validation.}
LLaMA-3.1-8B (Meta; d000, d025, d100) is classified Immune on both MMLU ($-$0.69pp) and ARC ($+$0.09pp), despite matching OLMo-2's Benefit baseline (53.5\% vs.\ 53.8\%)---demonstrating family-level factors beyond baseline ability modulate vulnerability. LLaMA-3.1 shows the strongest GSM8K transfer ($+$16.4pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:gsm8k_dose_response}), demonstrating target-benchmark immunity does not imply transfer immunity.

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Transfer}
\label{sec:gsm8k_transfer}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\columnwidth]{figures/fig_cross_benchmark_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Cross-benchmark transfer at d100. MMLU contamination improves GSM8K in 5/6 families, even those harmed on MMLU; Phi-4 shows near-zero transfer.}
\label{fig:cross_benchmark_heatmap}
\end{figure}

Contamination patterns are a property of the (model, benchmark) pair, not the model alone. Within MCQ format, MMLU--ARC direction agreement is 86\% (6/7 families; Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}); across formats (MCQ$\to$generative math), agreement drops to 40\% (2/5) with three families exhibiting directional reversal on GSM8K \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} (Table~\ref{tab:gsm8k_transfer}, Appendix). All five non-excluded families show positive GSM8K transfer ($+$1 to $+$16pp), including Qwen-14B ($-$9.4pp MMLU yet $+$13.1pp GSM8K), suggesting capability elicitation rather than memorization, consistent with null DiD (\S\ref{sec:memorization_test}). Volume effects are ruled out: d005 ($+$1.5\% data) yields $+$11.1pp for Qwen-7B, while Phi-4 shows near-zero transfer.

\subsection{Cross-Seed Stability}
\label{sec:cross_seed}

Benefit replication varies in statistical strength: Gemma-2's BCa 95\% CI ($[+1.49, +3.14]$) excludes zero; Falcon3 is directionally consistent ($n\!=\!2$, both positive); OLMo-2's CI ($[-0.55, +2.60]$) crosses zero (borderline). Parametric model comparison (Appendix~\ref{sec:parametric_dr}) indicates Gemma-2 and OLMo-2 dose-response curves are indistinguishable from a constant model by AICc, suggesting Benefit concentrates at d100 rather than following graded dose-response. Across families, direction agreement reaches 5/6 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}, Appendix); strict sign consistency is 3/6.

\subsection{Item-Level Memorization Test}
\label{sec:memorization_test}

We test whether dose-response patterns arise from item-specific memorization using the treated/untreated split at d025 (3{,}540 / 10{,}502 MMLU items). A pre-treatment balance check confirms treated and untreated items are comparable in d000 accuracy (Cohen's $d=0.02$), difficulty distribution, and domain coverage (all $p>0.19$), supporting the parallel trends assumption. No model shows significant DiD ($p > 0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:did}), ruling out item-specific effects $>$2pp (MDES 1.25--2.05pp; ARC MDES in Appendix~\ref{sec:mdes}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Treated vs.\ untreated item accuracy changes at d025 MMLU (3{,}540 / 10{,}502). All DiD non-significant. $^{**}p<.01$; $^{***}p<.001$; ns: $p>.05$.}
\label{tab:did}
\footnotesize
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Model & Treated $\Delta$ & Untreated $\Delta$ & DiD & Verdict \\
\midrule
Gemma-2 9B & $+$3.1$^{**}$ & $+$2.6$^{***}$ & $+$0.5 (ns) & generalization \\
OLMo-2 7B & $+$1.6 (ns) & $+$2.7$^{***}$ & $-$1.1 (ns) & mixed \\
Phi-4 14.7B & $+$0.3 (ns) & $+$0.6 (ns) & $-$0.3 (ns) & no-effect \\
Qwen-14B & $-$6.9$^{***}$ & $-$7.6$^{***}$ & $+$0.7 (ns) & anti-contam. \\
Qwen-7B & $-$3.8$^{**}$ & $-$2.9$^{***}$ & $-$0.8 (ns) & anti-contam. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Parallel changes suggest global capability modulation (Figure~\ref{fig:treated_untreated}, Appendix).

\paragraph{Volume control.}
A volume-matched control (Falcon3-7B trained on 66{,}533 non-benchmark MCQ items; Appendix~\ref{sec:volume_control}) achieves 60.67\% MMLU, decomposing the $+$4.19pp Benefit into data quantity ($+$1.90pp, 45\%) and contamination content ($+$2.29pp, 55\%); ARC yields a consistent 43\%/57\% split. Collapse ($-$9.4pp) and Immune ($<$1pp) cannot arise from volume effects that produce $+$1.90pp in the control.

\subsection{Calibration Quality}
\label{sec:calibration_results}

Within-family IRT analysis uses Qwen2.5 $\leq$3B ($J=16$), where aggregate contamination shifts accuracy by ${\sim}$0.6--0.7pp. IRT is fitted on a random subsample of 300 items (2.1\% of MMLU; seed$=$42); exposure scores for each item are derived from the RRF signal pipeline (\S\ref{sec:stage1}). The subsample is representative: KS test on item difficulty ($p = 0.946$) and $\chi^2$ test on domain distribution ($p = 0.574$) confirm no selection bias; all effect sizes $< 0.05$. On this subsample, RRF-based exposure scoring achieves $\tau = 0.73$ ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random; signal ablation in Appendix~\ref{sec:rrf_ablation}). An end-to-end comparison against a single-signal (Min-K\%++) pipeline shows severe model misfit (PPP $< 0.001$ vs.\ $0.193$; Appendix~\ref{sec:s1_only_pipeline}), confirming that multi-signal fusion contributes essential exposure-ranking quality.

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods on 300 randomly subsampled items with RRF exposure scores. Raw accuracy achieves lower MAE because contamination effects at observed dosages are small; ContamCal's value lies in domain-level diagnosis rather than aggregate score correction. B6-DCR systematically overcorrects (MAE 0.108). Social Sciences $\gamma_d = -0.63$ ($P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$; \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}) while pooled $\gamma$ remains non-significant, illustrating how opposing domain effects cancel in aggregate. Model fit diagnostics (PPP, CI coverage) are in Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity} and~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{MMLU calibration on 300 randomly subsampled items (Qwen2.5, $J$=16). ARC results in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & MAE & $\tau$ \\
\midrule
B0: Raw Accuracy & \textbf{0.006} & 0.767 \\
B1b: Remove \& Rescore & 0.026 & \underline{0.783} \\
B3: Vanilla 2PL & 0.008 & 0.731 \\
B4: Linear Regression & 0.010 & 0.751 \\
B5b: Emperor & \textbf{0.006} & \textbf{0.789} \\
B6: DCR (reimpl.) & 0.108 & 0.654 \\
ContamCal (2PL+$\gamma$) & 0.009 & 0.731 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
\label{sec:signal_failure}

Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits \textit{directional reversal} at 0.5B: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores (paired Cohen's $d = -1.98$ at d100, $|d| > 1.4$ at d005). S1 achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at all scales; S4 is near-random below 1.5B (Appendix~\ref{sec:signal_reliability_appendix}). Simulation shows $\gamma$ recovery requires AUC$\geq$0.55 with $J \geq 50$; at AUC $\geq 0.90$, $J = 16$ suffices (Appendix~\ref{sec:identifiability_appendix}).

\subsection{Domain-Level Analysis}
\label{sec:domain_differential}

Within-family domain analysis (Qwen2.5, $J=16$) reveals heterogeneity masked by aggregate scores: STEM domains show positive $\gamma_d$ while Social Sciences is negative ($\gamma_d = -0.63$, 95\% CI $[-1.07, -0.19]$, $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, PPP$=$0.722; Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}, Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma} in Appendix). Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0)$ is 99.35--99.85\% across priors (Table~\ref{tab:socsci_prior}), exceeding the Bonferroni threshold ($99.375\%$) under baseline and loose priors but falling 0.03\,pp short under the tight prior. The directional signal is robust; we treat domain-level findings as exploratory pending replication with larger ensembles.

